::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q01::[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Sources show senior transition workshops help when students use realistic examples, receive feedback, and revise. Which synthesis is strongest?
{
=B. Transition workshops are most useful when they combine realistic tasks, feedback, and revision time.#Correct. This connects all source ideas into one synthesis.
~A. Transition workshops exist in schools.#Incorrect. This does not synthesize source relationships.
~C. Feedback is unrelated to student preparation.#Incorrect. Feedback is central in the source set.
~D. Students should avoid revision.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source set.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q02::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which claim best follows from the transition workshop source set?
{
~B. Schools should end all senior support.#Incorrect. This ignores the evidence.
=A. Schools should provide senior transition workshops that include realistic tasks and revision after feedback.#Correct. This is specific, arguable, and evidence-based.
~C. Workshops are perfect in every situation.#Incorrect. This overstates the evidence.
~D. Students never need examples.#Incorrect. The sources say realistic examples help.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q03::[MLA.ENG4.W.03] Passage detail: marked drafts and an open door remain after a workshop. Which analysis is strongest?
{
~A. The room has papers.#Incorrect. This summarizes a detail.
~B. The projector is off.#Incorrect. This identifies a detail without explaining meaning.
=C. The details present revision as an ongoing process of preparation and growth.#Correct. This explains function and meaning.
~D. The workshop happened indoors.#Incorrect. This is literal, not analytical.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q04::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Source note: Students revised materials after feedback. Which paraphrase is accurate?
{
~A. Students refused all feedback.#Incorrect. This reverses the source.
=D. Students improved their materials after receiving responses from others.#Correct. This preserves the meaning in new wording.
~C. Students only listened to lectures.#Incorrect. This changes the source.
~B. Students never revised their work.#Incorrect. This contradicts the source.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q05::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which revision improves "The sources are good"?
{
~A. The sources are good good good.#Incorrect. Repetition does not improve writing.
=B. The sources show that workshops are strongest when students practice realistic tasks and revise after feedback.#Correct. This revision adds precise meaning.
~C. The sources say things.#Incorrect. This remains vague.
~D. Good sources are good.#Incorrect. This is circular.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q06::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence is clearest?
{
~C. Workshops because students and tasks and feedback.#Incorrect. This is fragmented.
~B. The workshop, feedback, revisions, because students.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
=A. Workshops can support seniors when they include realistic examples, feedback, and revision time.#Correct. This is clear and grammatically complete.
~D. Students things for after graduation.#Incorrect. This is vague and incomplete.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q07::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Which response best fits an academic checkpoint?
{
=D. State the claim, use evidence, and explain reasoning in a professional tone.#Correct. This matches academic communication expectations.
~B. Use slang and omit evidence.#Incorrect. This does not fit the audience or task.
~C. Submit only a title.#Incorrect. This does not communicate mastery.
~A. Avoid explaining the response.#Incorrect. Explanation is required.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q08::[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Sources show a policy has benefits but requires staffing. Which synthesis is strongest?
{
~A. Staffing concerns mean the policy has no benefits.#Incorrect. This ignores benefits.
=C. The policy may be useful when staffing needs are planned before implementation.#Correct. This connects benefit and condition.
~B. Benefits mean staffing never matters.#Incorrect. This ignores the concern.
~D. The sources are unrelated.#Incorrect. They address the same policy.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q09::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Evidence: A survey found seniors lacked quiet work time after school. Which reasoning best supports later library hours?
{
~A. Quiet work time is unrelated to applications.#Incorrect. It can directly affect application completion.
~C. The survey proves every student needs the same schedule.#Incorrect. This overstates the evidence.
=B. Later library hours could provide a structured space for students who lack quiet time elsewhere.#Correct. This explains how evidence supports the claim.
~D. Libraries should close earlier.#Incorrect. This does not follow from the evidence.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q10::[MLA.ENG4.W.03] Passage: "Numbers showed the storm's size. The sentences showed where it had touched a life." Which analysis is strongest?
{
=A. The contrast shows that data can measure a storm while personal records reveal human impact.#Correct. This explains function and meaning.
~B. The passage says numbers exist.#Incorrect. This is too literal.
~C. The passage rejects all data.#Incorrect. It says the point was useful.
~D. The passage is only about handwriting.#Incorrect. The contrast is broader.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q11::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which revision strengthens argument elaboration?
{
~A. This evidence is good because it is evidence.#Incorrect. This is circular.
=D. This evidence matters because it shows students need applied practice, not only definitions.#Correct. This explains significance.
~C. Evidence is in the paragraph.#Incorrect. This does not develop reasoning.
~B. The claim is good.#Incorrect. This is vague.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q12::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence avoids a comma splice?
{
~A. The sources show benefits, they also show challenges.#Incorrect. This is a comma splice.
=C. The sources show benefits, but they also identify practical challenges.#Correct. The conjunction correctly joins the clauses.
~B. The sources show benefits, challenges.#Incorrect. This is unclear.
~D. The sources, benefits and challenges.#Incorrect. This is fragmented.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q13::[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] What should happen before writing a synthesis statement?
{
=B. Understand and paraphrase each source accurately.#Correct. Accurate understanding comes before synthesis.
~A. Ignore sources that complicate the claim.#Incorrect. Complications can improve synthesis.
~C. Choose an opinion before reading.#Incorrect. Evidence should guide the claim.
~D. Copy sources without understanding.#Incorrect. Copying is not synthesis.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q14::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which claim is too broad for one city pilot program?
{
~B. The pilot suggests placement affects station use.#Incorrect. This is appropriately limited.
=A. Every city in the world must build stations immediately.#Correct. This overstates what one pilot can support.
~C. The city should review placement before expansion.#Incorrect. This follows from the pilot.
~D. Stations near transit stops may be useful.#Incorrect. This is limited and evidence-based.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q15::[MLA.ENG4.W.03] Which answer is analysis rather than summary?
{
~A. Mason works alone in a workshop.#Incorrect. This summarizes.
=D. Mason's revision of the ramp shows that responsible design must serve real people, not only appear impressive.#Correct. This explains author choice and meaning.
~C. The parking lot lights turn on.#Incorrect. This identifies a detail.
~B. The plans are on a table.#Incorrect. This summarizes setting.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q16::[MLA.ENG4.R.10] Which paraphrase preserves meaning?
{
~A. Original: Response times sometimes slowed. Paraphrase: Response times always stopped completely.#Incorrect. This exaggerates.
=C. Original: Response times sometimes slowed. Paraphrase: Communication was occasionally delayed.#Correct. This preserves meaning.
~B. Original: Response times sometimes slowed. Paraphrase: Communication became perfect.#Incorrect. This reverses the source.
~D. Original: Response times sometimes slowed. Paraphrase: No one communicated.#Incorrect. This overstates the source.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q17::[MLA.ENG4.C.01] Which digital communication practice is strongest?
{
=B. Use a clear subject, concise explanation, evidence when needed, and a professional tone.#Correct. This supports audience and purpose.
~A. Send unclear messages with no context.#Incorrect. This weakens communication.
~C. Use only emojis for a formal checkpoint.#Incorrect. This is not appropriate for the task.
~D. Leave the main point unstated.#Incorrect. Clear communication states the point.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q18::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which revision improves analysis?
{
~B. The passage has details and stuff.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=A. The author uses the open door and marked drafts to show that revision continues beyond the workshop session.#Correct. This explains detail and function.
~C. The passage is about a room.#Incorrect. This is summary.
~D. It is interesting.#Incorrect. This is personal reaction, not analysis.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q19::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which sentence best explains consequence?
{
~A. The source was a survey.#Incorrect. This is context, not consequence.
=D. Without applied practice, students may face adult decisions without knowing how to compare real costs.#Correct. This explains a possible result.
~C. The word credit has six letters.#Incorrect. This is irrelevant.
~B. Financial literacy is a phrase.#Incorrect. This does not explain consequence.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q20::[MLA.ENG4.W.03] What makes an analytical claim strong?
{
=C. It names a text choice and explains its meaning or effect.#Correct. This is the core of analysis.
~B. It only retells events.#Incorrect. That is summary.
~A. It avoids evidence.#Incorrect. Claims need evidence.
~D. It uses vague words.#Incorrect. Precision matters.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q21::[MLA.ENG4.RS.01] Which source relationship supports a qualified claim?
{
=B. Two sources show benefits, while one identifies a condition for success.#Correct. This supports a claim with limits.
~A. All sources are unrelated.#Incorrect. Unrelated sources cannot support synthesis.
~C. The writer ignores complications.#Incorrect. Ignoring complications weakens research.
~D. One source is copied without understanding.#Incorrect. Copying is not synthesis.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q22::[MLA.ENG4.W.02] Which option is reasoning rather than evidence?
{
~B. A survey found students lacked quiet work time.#Incorrect. This is evidence.
=A. This evidence matters because quiet work time affects students' ability to complete detailed applications.#Correct. This explains evidence.
~C. A report listed application completion rates.#Incorrect. This is evidence.
~D. A source stated a staffing concern.#Incorrect. This is evidence.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q23::[MLA.ENG4.L.01] Which sentence uses precise academic language?
{
~A. The stuff shows things.#Incorrect. This is vague.
=D. The evidence reveals a practical gap between knowing terms and applying them to decisions.#Correct. This uses precise academic language.
~C. It is good because good.#Incorrect. This is circular.
~B. There are many ideas or whatever.#Incorrect. This is informal.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q24::[MLA.ENG4.W.04] Which revision question is most useful?
{
~A. Can I make the sentence more vague?#Incorrect. Revision should improve precision.
=C. Does my explanation show why the evidence matters?#Correct. This checks development.
~B. Can I delete all evidence?#Incorrect. Evidence is required.
~D. Can I avoid the task?#Incorrect. Revision should address the task.
}
::ENG4_U04_ASMT_Q25::[MLA.ENG4.W.03] Which statement best distinguishes summary from analysis?
{
=B. Summary says what happens; analysis explains how author choices create meaning.#Correct. This clearly distinguishes the two.
~A. Summary and analysis are identical.#Incorrect. They serve different purposes.
~C. Analysis avoids the text.#Incorrect. Analysis uses text evidence.
~D. Summary explains author choices in depth.#Incorrect. That describes analysis.
}
